# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the clang runtimes (macOS).
#
# The clang ASan runtime reports a few allocations that macOS itself makes and that no program
# can free: the Objective-C runtime's class list, dyld's thread-local storage, and the buffer
# that Apple's libc uses to format a floating-point number. Each entry below matches only the
# leaks whose allocation stack passes through that platform frame, so a leak in our own code is
# still reported (verified: a deliberate 1234 byte leak is still reported with this file).
#
# The GCC runtime needs none of this, because its leak check cannot run on macOS at all: it
# deadlocks inside libasan's StopTheWorld at exit, in a two-line program as well (see
# docs/asan-leak-check/README.md).
#
# See docs/asan-leak-check/README.md for the analysis and the measurements behind every entry.

# libobjc's class initialization: _fetchInitializingClassList -> initializeNonMetaClass.
leak:libobjc

# dyld's thread-local storage: any thread_local makes dyld allocate the block for the slot.
leak:libdyld
leak:_dyld_shared_cache_iterate_text

# Apple's libc allocates a buffer to format a floating-point number (snprintf "%f", "%g", ...).
leak:snprintf
leak:_vfprintf
